{"post_stream": {"posts": [{"id": 275379, "name": "Aleksandra Fedorova", "username": "bookwar", "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/b/51a2da/{size}.png", "created_at": "2024-02-12T11:22:04.230Z", "cooked": "<h1><a name=\"p-275379-intro-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-275379-intro-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"></a>Intro</h1>\n<p>One of the main topics discussed at the CentOS Connect in Brussels last week was the ability for third-parties (for example RDO or IdM teams) to contribute test results to the <a href=\"https://sigs.centos.org/integration/architecture/#three-gates\">Compose Gate</a> of the CentOS Stream so that they can prevent regressions on CentOS Stream packages before they hit the public mirrors.</p>\n<p>Currently we run the so called t_functional test suite in the Compose gate, which can be extended by contributing your own test cases (the TMT version of the test suite is being developed in <a href=\"https://gitlab.com/CentOS/Integration/compose-tests\" class=\"inline-onebox\">CentOS / Integration SIG / compose-tests · GitLab</a>)</p>\n<p>But for larger projects like <a href=\"https://www.rdoproject.org/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">RDO (Openstack in RPMs)</a> or <a href=\"https://www.freeipa.org\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">IdM (Identity Management)</a> test cases might be too complex or too specialized to be run in a generic test environment.</p>\n<p>Thus we’d like to provide the ability for third-parties to get the latest nightly compose, test it on their own environment and contribute test results back to the CentOS RelEng team.</p>\n<p>Technically speaking main pieces for this workflow are already available:<br>\nnightly composes are published in the open at <a href=\"https://composes.stream.centos.org/production/latest-CentOS-Stream/compose/\" class=\"inline-onebox\">Index of /production/latest-CentOS-Stream/compose</a> and the message is sent to Fedora Message Bus or can be picked from the <a href=\"https://testing.stream.centos.org/job/CentOS_9-stream_all/api/json\">Testing Jenkins API</a>, but it does not provide the visibility and requires custom tooling.</p>\n<p>During the CentOS Integration SIG Meetup we discussed that we’d like to provide the Git interface to the Compose gate so that <strong>each new compose is represented as a new Merge Request to a Git repository</strong>.</p>\n<h1><a name=\"p-275379-proposed-workflow-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-275379-proposed-workflow-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"></a>Proposed Workflow</h1>\n<p>There is a new git repository (Compose Repo), in the Integration SIG space.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>New compose is created by the CentOS Stream Infra</li>\n<li>Automation is triggered by the new compose event. It generates new Merge Request to the Compose Repo (the content of the MR is TBD, but it can be for example a list of NVRs included in the compose)</li>\n<li>Third-parties watch for the new merge request in the Compose Repo and trigger their own tests on the new compose</li>\n<li>Once they are done with the testing, they report test results on the Merge Request via votes/labels/comments/TBD..</li>\n<li>As soon as compose is verified and tested, we promote it to be published to the CentOS Mirrors</li>\n<li>Automation recognizes the publishing event and merges the relevant Merge Request to the repository</li>\n</ul>\n<h1><a name=\"p-275379-notes-3\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-275379-notes-3\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"></a>Notes</h1>\n<p>On the first iteration we will use Git repo only as a representation of the existing pipeline which runs in the background. The Merge Request will be created slightly <em>after</em> the compose is built by the build system and merged slightly <em>after</em> the compose is pushed to the mirrors.</p>\n<p>This way we can play with the idea on the side without disrupting the main compose workflow.<br>\nOnce we figure out the format for Merge Request and Compose Repo content, we may eventually switch the workflow so that Merge Request becomes the main entry point for the compose pipeline.</p>\n<h1><a name=\"p-275379-whats-next-4\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-275379-whats-next-4\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"></a>What’s next</h1>\n<p>This is currently the outline of the idea.</p>\n<p>We will track the work on this topic in <a href=\"https://gitlab.com/groups/CentOS/Integration/-/epics/2\">the dedicated Epic</a> in the CentOS Integration SIG GitLab space.</p>\n<p>Please join us bringing your feedback, questions, suggestions and, of course, contributions <img src=\"https://emoji.discourse-cdn.com/twemoji/slight_smile.png?v=15\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>", "post_number": 1, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 5, "updated_at": "2024-02-12T11:22:04.230Z", "reply_count": 0, "reply_to_post_number": null, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 77, "reads": 38, "readers_count": 37, "score": 437.6, "yours": false, "topic_id": 104981, "topic_slug": "centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate", "display_username": "Aleksandra Fedorova", "primary_group_name": "council", "flair_name": "council", "flair_url": "https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/original/2X/3/312b3d4695e3ffa99ceffdbbf5c1f78b7560de47.png", "flair_bg_color": "", "flair_color": "", "flair_group_id": 43, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "link_counts": [{"url": "https://gitlab.com/groups/CentOS/Integration/-/epics/2", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "Git for the Compose Gate (&2) · Epics · Integration SIG · GitLab", "clicks": 4}, {"url": "https://composes.stream.centos.org/production/latest-CentOS-Stream/compose/", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "Index of /production/latest-CentOS-Stream/compose", "clicks": 1}, {"url": "https://gitlab.com/CentOS/Integration/compose-tests", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "CentOS / Integration SIG / compose-tests · GitLab", "clicks": 1}, {"url": "https://www.rdoproject.org/", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "RDO", "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://www.freeipa.org", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "FreeIPA - Identity, Policy, Audit — FreeIPA documentation", "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://sigs.centos.org/integration/architecture/#three-gates", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "Architecture - CentOS Integration SIG", "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://testing.stream.centos.org/job/CentOS_9-stream_all/api/json", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://discussion.fedoraproject.org/t/council-election-interview-with-aleksandra-fedorova-bookwar/117520", "internal": true, "reflection": true, "title": "Council Election: Interview with Aleksandra Fedorova (bookwar)", "clicks": 0}], "read": true, "user_title": "Council Member", "title_is_group": false, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [{"id": 2, "count": 3}], "moderator": false, "admin": true, "staff": true, "user_id": 376, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 3, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate/104981/1", "event": null, "calendar_details": [], "reactions": [{"id": "heart", "type": "emoji", "count": 3}, {"id": "eureka", "type": "emoji", "count": 1}], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 4, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": null}, {"id": 275653, "name": "Timothée Ravier", "username": "siosm", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/siosm/{size}/79_2.png", "created_at": "2024-02-13T13:57:38.120Z", "cooked": "<p>From my perspective, a compose should be a set of lockfiles that determine which versions of which packages from a repository are bundled all together: <a href=\"https://github.com/rpm-software-management/dnf5/issues/833\" class=\"inline-onebox\">build system dependency management / lockfile support · Issue #833 · rpm-software-management/dnf5 · GitHub</a></p>\n<p>Maybe this is how we should represent things in this Git repo. This is what we use in Fedora CoreOS to lock the versions of packages in our builds.</p>\n<p>In Fedora land, we (Fedora CoreOS) are looking at integrating into <a href=\"https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/ResultsDB\">ResultsDB</a> for test results.</p>", "post_number": 2, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 5, "updated_at": "2024-02-13T13:58:55.249Z", "reply_count": 1, "reply_to_post_number": null, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 28, "readers_count": 27, "score": 10.6, "yours": false, "topic_id": 104981, "topic_slug": "centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate", "display_username": "Timothée Ravier", "primary_group_name": "packager", "flair_name": "packager", "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": "", "flair_color": "", "flair_group_id": 68, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "link_counts": [{"url": "https://github.com/rpm-software-management/dnf5/issues/833", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "build system dependency management / lockfile support · Issue #833 · rpm-software-management/dnf5 · GitHub", "clicks": 2}, {"url": "https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/ResultsDB", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "ResultsDB - Fedora Project Wiki", "clicks": 0}], "read": true, "user_title": "Fedora Engineering Steering Committee", "title_is_group": false, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [], "moderator": false, "admin": false, "staff": false, "user_id": 90, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 3, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate/104981/2", "event": null, "reactions": [], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 0, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": null}, {"id": 275957, "name": "Aleksandra Fedorova", "username": "bookwar", "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/b/51a2da/{size}.png", "created_at": "2024-02-14T18:41:24.284Z", "cooked": "<p>This looks interesting, but there are 2 concerns:</p>\n<h2><a name=\"p-275957-h-1-deps-file-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-275957-h-1-deps-file-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"></a>1. deps file</h2>\n<p>If we put the entire koji tag in one file, it becomes the constant source of merge conflicts and generally a pain to read. So it would be nice to have some way to use /rpm/deps.d/* as an alternative</p>\n<h2><a name=\"p-275957-h-2-builds-vs-rpms-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-275957-h-2-builds-vs-rpms-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"></a>2. Builds vs rpms</h2>\n<p>This is a larger issue, which is honestly a big pain for the entire distro development process:</p>\n<p>Our development pipeline, updates, koji tags, composes, etc operate on rpm <em>builds</em> not rpm <em>packages</em>. While dnf doesn’t have a concept of a build, as it operates on binary RPMs. Thus from the RPM/dnf side dependency trees for x86 or aarch64 are two different things, while from the pipeline perspective we don’t push update to mirrors if it fails on any of the architectures we support.</p>\n<p>So in the proposal we need to track composes as sets of rpm <em>builds</em>. We can not treat them as individual arch-dependent binary rpm packages.</p>", "post_number": 3, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 5, "updated_at": "2024-02-14T19:48:33.117Z", "reply_count": 1, "reply_to_post_number": 2, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 26, "readers_count": 25, "score": 10.2, "yours": false, "topic_id": 104981, "topic_slug": "centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate", "display_username": "Aleksandra Fedorova", "primary_group_name": "council", "flair_name": "council", "flair_url": "https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/fedoraproject/original/2X/3/312b3d4695e3ffa99ceffdbbf5c1f78b7560de47.png", "flair_bg_color": "", "flair_color": "", "flair_group_id": 43, "badges_granted": [], "version": 2, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "read": true, "user_title": "Council Member", "title_is_group": false, "reply_to_user": {"id": 90, "username": "siosm", "name": "Timothée Ravier", "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discussion.fedoraproject.org/siosm/{size}/79_2.png"}, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [], "moderator": false, "admin": true, "staff": true, "user_id": 376, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 3, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [], "post_url": "/t/centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate/104981/3", "event": null, "reactions": [], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 0, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": null}, {"id": 277305, "name": "Liora Milbaum", "username": "lmilbaum", "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/l/b193c8/{size}.png", "created_at": "2024-02-20T11:03:16.924Z", "cooked": "<p><a class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/bookwar\">@bookwar</a> Thank you so much for driving this effort. I really like where it is going.<br>\nThere is an existing effort to standardize a rpm lockfile and implementing tools which takes a input file consists of list of bare rpms and outputs the lockfile. I’d suggest to take it into consideration when engineering this request.<br>\nI’ve also mentioned of the Epic that I’ve already implemented a sub-set of these effort with using the open source Renovate project.</p>", "post_number": 4, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 5, "updated_at": "2024-02-20T11:03:16.924Z", "reply_count": 0, "reply_to_post_number": 3, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 23, "readers_count": 22, "score": 4.6, "yours": false, "topic_id": 104981, "topic_slug": "centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate", "display_username": "Liora Milbaum", "primary_group_name": null, "flair_name": null, "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": null, "flair_color": null, "flair_group_id": null, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "read": true, "user_title": null, "reply_to_user": {"id": 376, "username": "bookwar", "name": "Aleksandra Fedorova", "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/b/51a2da/{size}.png"}, "bookmarked": false, "actions_summary": [], "moderator": false, "admin": false, "staff": false, "user_id": 18025, "hidden": false, "trust_level": 1, "deleted_at": null, "user_deleted": false, "edit_reason": null, "can_view_edit_history": true, "wiki": false, "mentioned_users": [{"id": 376, "username": "bookwar", "name": "Aleksandra Fedorova", "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/b/51a2da/{size}.png"}], "post_url": "/t/centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate/104981/4", "event": null, "reactions": [], "current_user_reaction": null, "reaction_users_count": 0, "current_user_used_main_reaction": false, "can_accept_answer": false, "can_unaccept_answer": false, "accepted_answer": false, "topic_accepted_answer": null}, {"id": 282116, "name": "Alfredo Moralejo", "username": "amoralej", "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/a/3c6eb4/{size}.png", "created_at": "2024-03-12T12:55:22.499Z", "cooked": "<p>Thanks for summarizing and sharing!</p>\n<p>Some open questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What jobs could be blockers for the publishing events?</li>\n<li>During the centos connect meeting we were discussing also about being able to create different combinations of CentOS repos for specific cases based on those definition files (i.e. latest content which passed RDO CI jobs). Will that be on the scope of this epic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In RDO we use similar approach to manage our RPMs repos content and some other metadata (mostly used for tooling). I think it’s wothy to share some of our experience. The in-files db can be found in <a href=\"https://github.com/redhat-openstack/rdoinfo\" class=\"inline-onebox\">GitHub - redhat-openstack/rdoinfo: DO NOT send Pull Requests here, send reviews to https://review.rdoproject.org/r/#/q/project:rdoinfo</a></p>\n<p>We use a python library ( <a href=\"https://github.com/softwarefactory-project/distroinfo\" class=\"inline-onebox\">GitHub - softwarefactory-project/distroinfo: python module for parsing, validating and querying distribution/packaging metadata stored in human readable and reviewable text/YAML files</a> ) to parse and apply some logic to merge the content of the different files in rdoinfo . This allows us to have separate files for each independent repo (based on openstack and centos release, and promotion phase, see <a href=\"https://github.com/redhat-openstack/rdoinfo/tree/master/buildsys-tags\" class=\"inline-onebox\">rdoinfo/buildsys-tags at master · redhat-openstack/rdoinfo · GitHub</a> ).</p>\n<p>It has been great to have a clean rebases without conflicts and without merge commits. Also we have rdopkg tool using distroinfo parser and rdoinfo repo.</p>\n<p>We also use CI on reviews (gerrit based) to do proper CI before updating packages in repos etc… (however we don’t use pungi and composes but purely cbs tagging which is in post pipeline).</p>\n<p>Some example reviews:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://review.rdoproject.org/r/c/rdoinfo/+/52051\" class=\"onebox\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">https://review.rdoproject.org/r/c/rdoinfo/+/52051</a><br>\n<a href=\"https://review.rdoproject.org/r/c/rdoinfo/+/51427\" class=\"onebox\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">https://review.rdoproject.org/r/c/rdoinfo/+/51427</a></p>\n<p>I hope this is useful.</p>", "post_number": 5, "post_type": 1, "posts_count": 5, "updated_at": "2024-03-12T12:55:22.499Z", "reply_count": 0, "reply_to_post_number": null, "quote_count": 0, "incoming_link_count": 0, "reads": 21, "readers_count": 20, "score": 19.2, "yours": false, "topic_id": 104981, "topic_slug": "centos-connect-follow-up-proposal-for-the-git-interface-to-the-compose-gate", "display_username": "Alfredo Moralejo", "primary_group_name": null, "flair_name": null, "flair_url": null, "flair_bg_color": null, "flair_color": null, "flair_group_id": null, "badges_granted": [], "version": 1, "can_edit": false, "can_delete": false, "can_recover": false, "can_see_hidden_post": false, "can_wiki": false, "link_counts": [{"url": "https://review.rdoproject.org/r/c/rdoinfo/+/52051", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "clicks": 2}, {"url": "https://review.rdoproject.org/r/c/rdoinfo/+/51427", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://github.com/redhat-openstack/rdoinfo/tree/master/buildsys-tags", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "rdoinfo/buildsys-tags at master · redhat-openstack/rdoinfo · GitHub", "clicks": 0}, {"url": "https://github.com/softwarefactory-project/distroinfo", "internal": false, "reflection": false, "title": "GitHub - softwarefactory-project/distroinfo: python module for parsing, validating and querying distribution/packaging metadata stored in human readable and reviewable text/YAML files"